;;; `std.bijection` — `bijective-setup`'s two activators of the obligations,
;;; `total-owed` and `surjective-owed`.
;;;
;;; ── What the rule promises ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   bijective-setup   (bijective R) + (bijection-hierarchy isa) concludes,
;;;                     among its eight activators, the two that make a state
;;;                     owe: `(total-owed R isa)`, every member of R's first
;;;                     label an image, and `(surjective-owed R isa)`, every
;;;                     member of its second a preimage
;;;
;;; ── What this program does to it ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two agents, two colours, one bijective relation over one hierarchy. Root's
;;; saturation concludes the two activators, each of `wears` over `is-a`, in
;;; that order: the relation that owes, then the hierarchy its labels are read
;;; through.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation means, and why it reads the activators ────────────
;;;
;;; Root owes, so R is empty and root is the node it stops at. The claim lists
;;; that node by the two activators it holds, which closes the held extent of
;;; each: exactly `wears` over `is-a`. An activator with its two arguments
;;; exchanged would name the hierarchy as the relation that owes, and root
;;; would hold another fact of it.
;;;
;;; No claim about R can say this. On a bijection the two obligations agree at
;;; every state: with a fact of the relation excluding its row and its column,
;;; a state gives every member of the first label an image exactly when it
;;; gives every member of the second a preimage, and what each obligation
;;; would propose is the same set of facts. So with one of the two activated
;;; for another relation, the other still makes every state owe what it owed,
;;; and R, the nodes that owe and the hypotheses are what they were. Where the
;;; two labels have different counts the elimination rules of the same setup
;;; end root before any state is asked. The activators are where the
;;; difference is, so that is what the claim reads.
(import std.algebra   :symbols (total-owed surjective-owed))
(import std.bijection)

(relation is-a  T T          :why "{?3} is a {?4}")
(relation wears Agent Colour :why "{?3} wears {?4}")

(is-a Ann Agent)  (is-a Bob Agent)
(is-a Red Colour) (is-a Green Colour)

(bijective wears)
(bijection-hierarchy is-a)

;; Root alone (Q-M1f.254): the question is what root's saturation concludes,
;; so nothing is guessed. Root owes, and is the owing node R stops at.
(solve
  :no-search
  :expect (region (owing (model (total-owed wears is-a)
                                (surjective-owed wears is-a)))))
